      "latency_ms": 2147.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 593,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **Chemical Bonds:** The atoms are held together by covalent bonds. Furthermore, water molecules form \"hydrogen bonds\" with each other, which gives water its unique properties (like high surface tension and the ability to dissolve many substances).\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The standard state we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which is unique because it is *less dense* as a solid than as a liquid (which is why ice floats).\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the form of an invisible gas in the atmosphere.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several bizarre and wonderful properties that make life possible:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical polarity, water can dissolve more substances (salt, sugar, minerals, nutrients) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients and how plants absorb food from the soil.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This regulates the climate of the Earth and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—creating drops and surface tension) and to other surfaces (adhesion—allowing water to travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 4. Ecological and Biological Importance\n* **On Earth:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth’s surface**, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water), but also in lakes, rivers, glaciers, underground aquifers, and the atmosphere.\n* **In Living Things:** The human body is made of roughly **60% water**. Brains are about 73% water, and lungs are about 83%. Without water, biochemical reactions within cells (metabolism) cannot happen, and organisms quickly dehydrate and die.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule made of hydrogen and oxygen, but its unique physics and chemistry make it the **lifeblood of our planet**—acting as a habitat, a temperature regulator, a transport system, and the fundamental medium of life itself."
